#! Temporal Displacement - Program Definition
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   A source file defining the semantic model, physical assumptions,
##   state representation, validation rules, result contracts, and
##   execution behavior for atomic temporal displacement of an Observer.
##
## HumanModel:
##   This program represents temporal displacement as a transition
##   between complete Observer states. The source code defines the
##   expected semantics and constraints required for a valid
##   displacement operation.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   Temporal displacement is an atomic physical transition. From the
##   Observer's perspective, the Observer exists in the source state
##   before displacement and in the destination state after successful
##   displacement. No observable intermediate Observer state exists.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   The implementation must preserve Observer state integrity,
##   validate proposed transitions before execution, and ensure that
##   successful displacement results in the complete destination state.
##
## DefinitionStatus:
##   Version 0.4 semantic model and interface definition.
!#

#! Observer - State Model
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   The Observer state is the complete representation of the Observer
##   at a specific point in spacetime.
##
## HumanModel:
##   An Observer state is represented as an indivisible collection of
##   temporal and spatial reference values describing the Observer's
##   complete physical state.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   An Observer state consists of the temporal position, spatial
##   position, and reference context required to uniquely identify the
##   Observer state.
##
## ExpectedState:
##   EpochTime:
##     The temporal position of the Observer.
##
##   ReferenceEntity:
##     The reference object or coordinate context used to define the
##     Observer's spatial relationship.
##
##   Coordinates:
##     The complete spatial position of the Observer relative to the
##     reference entity.
##
## StateIntegrity:
##   Observer state must be treated as a complete state. Partial state
##   mutation does not represent a valid temporal displacement.
!#

->lib:sin:external:TMTE

#! TemporalDisplacement - Interface
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   Defines the required behavior for atomic temporal displacement.
##
## HumanModel:
##   TemporalDisplacement defines the semantic contract for moving an
##   Observer between complete states. The underlying physical mechanism
##   is isolated behind the physical_transition() technology boundary.
!#
TemporalDisplacement:interface {
    displace(observer, target_state):function:external -> DisplacementResult
}

#! TemporalDisplacement - Internal Contract
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   Defines required internal behaviors necessary to satisfy the
##   TemporalDisplacement interface.
##
## HumanModel:
##   The displacement process requires validation of the proposed
##   destination state before attempting the atomic transition.
##
## RequiredBehavior:
##   validate_proposed_displacement():
##     Must determine whether the proposed displacement satisfies
##     all required constraints.
##
##   physical_transition():
##     Defines the technology boundary through which the physical
##     displacement mechanism may be invoked.
##
##   perform_atomic_transition():
##     Coordinates the logical transaction, invokes the physical
##     mechanism, and preserves the atomicity invariant.
!#
validate_current_state(observer):function:internal -> ValidationResult
validate_proposed_displacement(observer, target_state):function:internal -> ValidationResult
perform_atomic_transition(
    observer,
    target_state
):function:internal -> TransitionResult

#! ValidationResult - Record
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   An immutable description of the result of validating a proposed
##   displacement.
##
## HumanModel:
##   ValidationResult records whether a displacement proposal satisfies
##   required constraints.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   This record does not perform validation and cannot mutate state.
!#
ValidationResult:record {
    valid
    failure_reason
    measured_variance
    verification_details
}

#! PhysicalTransitionResult - Record
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   An immutable description of the result of attempting the atomic
##   transition between Observer states.
##
## HumanModel:
##   TransitionResult records the outcome of the physical displacement
##   attempt.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   This record does not execute transitions and cannot mutate state.
!#
PhysicalTransitionResult:record {
    available
    successful
    failure_reason
    evidence
}

#! TransitionResult - Record
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   An immutable description of the logical atomic transition
##   attempt between Observer states.
##
## HumanModel:
##   TransitionResult records the outcome of the logical
##   transaction that coordinates the physical transition
##   mechanism.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   A successful result means:
##
##     observer.state == destination_state
##
##   A failed result means:
##
##     observer.state == source_state
##
##   Any violation of those atomicity conditions is reported as
##   a transition failure.
##
## StateIntegrity:
##   This record does not execute transitions and cannot mutate
##   Observer state.
!#
TransitionResult:record {
    success
    source_state
    destination_state
    failure_reason
    physical_transition_result
}

#! DisplacementResult - Record
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   An immutable complete description of a temporal displacement result.
##
## HumanModel:
##   DisplacementResult combines validation and transition information
##   into one inspectable outcome.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   A displacement result must provide enough information to determine:
##     - whether displacement succeeded
##     - original source state
##     - requested destination state
##     - validation outcome
##     - transition outcome
##     - failure reason when applicable
##
## StateIntegrity:
##   Result records describe state transitions but do not modify them.
!#
DisplacementResult:record {
    success
    source_state
    destination_state
    failure_reason
    validation_result
    transition_result
}

#! Observer State Validation - Function
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   Evaluates whether the Observer's current state satisfies all
##   required structural and semantic constraints.
##
## HumanModel:
##   State validation determines whether the Observer currently contains
##   a complete and logically valid state under the current USF model.
##
## ValidationLayers:
##   1. Observer existence.
##   2. Observer state existence.
##   3. State member presence.
##   4. State member value validity.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   State validation does not perform or imply a physical displacement.
##
## StateIntegrity:
##   Observer state before validation must equal Observer state after
##   validation.
##
##   Validation must never modify observer.state.
!#
validate_current_state(observer):function:internal {

    #
    # Capture the current state before any validation work.
    #
    # Validation is strictly non-mutating. The captured state is used
    # as a defensive integrity check before returning.
    #

    current_state == observer.state

    if (!defined(observer)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Observer does not exist.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "State validation layer 1: observer existence failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(current_state)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Observer state does not exist.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "State validation layer 2: observer state existence failed."
        );
    }

    #
    # Layer 3 - State member presence.
    #
    # The current Observer state model requires:
    #   et
    #   reference_entity
    #   x
    #   y
    #   z
    #
    # Presence is determined with defined().
    #
    # This deliberately does not use truthiness because explicitly
    # present values such as 0 are valid candidates.
    #
    if (!defined(current_state.et)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Observer state is missing epoch time.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "State validation layer 3: epoch time presence failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(current_state.reference_entity)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Observer state has no reference entity.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "State validation layer 3: reference entity presence failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(current_state.x)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Observer state is missing x coordinate.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "State validation layer 3: x coordinate presence failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(current_state.y)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Observer state is missing y coordinate.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "State validation layer 3: y coordinate presence failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(current_state.z)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Observer state is missing z coordinate.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "State validation layer 3: z coordinate presence failed."
        );
    }

    #
    # Final non-mutation integrity check.
    #
    # validate_state() must never modify Observer state.
    #
    # If Observer state differs from the state captured at entry,
    # validation has violated its contract and the request must fail.
    #
    # This does NOT attempt to restore the state because validation
    # itself must not perform state mutation.
    #
    if (observer.state != current_state) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Observer state changed during validation.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "State validation integrity check failed: validation was not non-mutating."
        );
    }


    #
    # State validation succeeded.
    #
    # No physical transition has occurred.
    # The Observer contains a complete and valid current state.
    #
    ret ValidationResult(
        valid = true,
        failure_reason = null,
        measured_variance = null,
        verification_details = "Observer state validation passed."
    );
}

#! Observer Proposed Displacement Validation - Function
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   Evaluates whether a proposed displacement satisfies all required
##   constraints before execution.
##
## HumanModel:
##   Validation determines whether the source -> destination transition
##   is logically valid under the current USF model.
##
## ValidationLayers:
##   1. Input and source-state existence/integrity.
##   2. Source-state completeness.
##   3. Destination-state completeness.
##   4. Source/destination consistency and required reference/state data.
##   5. Domain-level displacement validity.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   Validation does not perform the physical displacement.
##
## StateIntegrity:
##   Observer state before validation must equal Observer state after
##   validation.
##
##   Validation must never modify observer.state.
!#
validate_proposed_displacement(observer, target_state):function:internal {

    #
    # Capture the source state before any validation work.
    #
    # Validation is strictly non-mutating. The captured state is used
    # as a defensive integrity check before returning.
    #
    if (!defined(observer)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Observer does not exist.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 1: observer existence failed."
        );
    }

    source_state = observer.state;

    if (!defined(source_state)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Observer source state does not exist.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 1: source state existence failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(target_state)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Destination state does not exist.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 1: destination state existence failed."
        );
    }


    #
    # Layer 2 - Source-state completeness.
    #
    # The current Observer state model requires:
    #   et
    #   reference_entity
    #   x
    #   y
    #   z
    #
    # Presence is tested with defined(). Value validity is tested
    # separately.
    #
    # Do not mutate or normalize missing values here.
    #
    if (!defined(source_state.et)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Source state is missing epoch time.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 2: source epoch time presence failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(source_state.reference_entity)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Source state has no reference entity.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 2: source reference entity failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(source_state.x)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Source state is missing x coordinate.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 2: source x coordinate failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(source_state.y)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Source state is missing y coordinate.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 2: source y coordinate failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(source_state.z)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Source state is missing z coordinate.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 2: source z coordinate failed."
        );
    }

    #
    # Layer 3 - Destination-state completeness.
    #
    # A destination is a complete Observer state. It is not merely a
    # collection of independent coordinate mutations.
    #
    if (!defined(target_state.et)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Destination state is missing epoch time.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 3: destination epoch time presence failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(target_state.reference_entity)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Destination state has no reference entity.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 3: destination reference entity failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(target_state.x)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Destination state is missing x coordinate.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 3: destination x coordinate failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(target_state.y)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Destination state is missing y coordinate.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 3: destination y coordinate failed."
        );
    }

    if (!defined(target_state.z)) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Destination state is missing z coordinate.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 3: destination z coordinate failed."
        );
    }

    #
    # Layer 4 - Source/destination consistency.
    #
    # The current state model requires both states to have the
    # reference information necessary to interpret their coordinates.
    #
    # A different reference entity is not automatically accepted as a
    # valid displacement because the current model does not yet define
    # cross-reference-frame transformation semantics.
    #
    if (source_state.reference_entity != target_state.reference_entity) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Source and destination reference entities differ.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 4: reference-state consistency failed."
        );
    }

    #
    # Layer 5 - Domain-level displacement validity.
    #
    # Negative temporal displacement is explicitly permitted.
    #
    # Therefore:
    #
    #     target_state.et < source_state.et
    #
    # is NOT itself a validation failure.
    #
    # A zero displacement is different. The operation must represent
    # an actual displacement, so an identical source/destination state
    # is rejected.
    #
    if (
        source_state.et == target_state.et &&
        source_state.x == target_state.x &&
        source_state.y == target_state.y &&
        source_state.z == target_state.z &&
        source_state.reference_entity == target_state.reference_entity
    ) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Zero displacement is not a valid displacement request.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation layer 5: displacement-required rule failed."
        );
    }

    #
    # Final non-mutation integrity check.
    #
    # validate_proposed_displacement() must never modify Observer state.
    #
    # If Observer state differs from the state captured at entry,
    # validation has violated its contract and the request must fail.
    #
    # This does NOT attempt to restore the state because validation
    # itself must not perform state mutation.
    #
    if (observer.state != source_state) {
        ret ValidationResult(
            valid = false,
            failure_reason = "Observer state changed during validation.",
            measured_variance = null,
            verification_details = "Validation integrity check failed: validation was not non-mutating."
        );
    }

    #
    # Validation succeeded.
    #
    # No physical transition has occurred.
    # Negative temporal displacement is allowed.
    # The destination is logically valid under the currently defined
    # state model.
    #
    ret ValidationResult(
        valid = true,
        failure_reason = null,
        measured_variance = null,
        verification_details = "All logical displacement validation layers passed."
    );
}

#! Physical Temporal Transition Mechanism - Function
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   Defines the technology boundary through which an actual
##   physical temporal displacement mechanism may be invoked.
##
## HumanModel:
##   The logical TemporalDisplacement implementation does not
##   invent or simulate the physical mechanism.
##
##   This function is the replaceable boundary between the
##   logical transaction and whatever physical technology may
##   eventually perform temporal displacement.
##
## CurrentImplementation:
##   No physical temporal displacement mechanism is currently
##   available to this experiment.
##
##   Therefore the current implementation explicitly reports
##   mechanism unavailability rather than simulating a transition.
##
## StateIntegrity:
##   When unavailable, this function must not modify Observer
##   state.
##
## Evidence:
##   The evidence field is intentionally untyped and
##   technology-defined. TemporalDisplacement treats it as
##   mechanism-provided opaque evidence.
!#
physical_transition(
    observer,
    source_state,
    destination_state
):function:internal -> PhysicalTransitionResult {

    ##
    ## Hypothetical physical mechanism:
    ##
    ##   Temporal Metric Translation Engine (TMTE)
    ##
    ## The TMTE is assumed to be a physically realized technology
    ## capable of engineering a temporary spacetime transition
    ## geometry Gamma_D connecting source_state to destination_state.
    ##
    ## This implementation does NOT assign Observer state directly.
    ## The physical apparatus performs the transition.
    ##

    ret device_result = TMTE.execute_transition(
        observer,
        source_state,
        destination_state
    );

    ##
    ## The mechanism itself must establish the destination state.
    ## Software accepts success only when independent verification
    ## confirms that the physical destination matches the requested
    ## destination.
    ##

    if (!device_result.available) {
        ret PhysicalTransitionResult(
            available = false,
            successful = false,
            failure_reason = device_result.failure_reason,
            evidence = device_result.evidence
        );
    }

    if (!device_result.successful) {
        ret PhysicalTransitionResult(
            available = true,
            successful = false,
            failure_reason = device_result.failure_reason,
            evidence = device_result.evidence
        );
    }

    if (!device_result.evidence.destination_state_verified) {
        ret PhysicalTransitionResult(
            available = true,
            successful = false,
            failure_reason = "Physical mechanism reported success but independent destination-state verification failed.",
            evidence = device_result.evidence
        );
    }

    if (!device_result.evidence.causal_connection_verified) {
        ret PhysicalTransitionResult(
            available = true,
            successful = false,
            failure_reason = "Physical mechanism reported success but causal transition was not independently verified.",
            evidence = device_result.evidence
        );
    }

    if (!device_result.evidence.temporal_order_verified) {
        ret PhysicalTransitionResult(
            available = true,
            successful = false,
            failure_reason = "Physical mechanism reported success but temporal ordering was not independently verified.",
            evidence = device_result.evidence
        );
    }

    if (!device_result.evidence.conservation_accounting_verified) {
        ret PhysicalTransitionResult(
            available = true,
            successful = false,
            failure_reason = "Physical mechanism reported success but conservation accounting was not independently verified.",
            evidence = device_result.evidence
        );
    }

    ret PhysicalTransitionResult(
        available = true,
        successful = true,
        failure_reason = null,
        evidence = device_result.evidence
    );
}

#! Observer Perform Atomic Transition - Function
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   Coordinates the logical atomic transaction between the
##   Observer's source state and the requested destination state.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   A successful atomic transition establishes destination_state.
##
##   A failed atomic transition preserves source_state.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   1. Capture the source state.
##   2. Invoke the physical transition mechanism.
##   3. Distinguish mechanism availability from mechanism success.
##   4. Verify the atomicity contract after the mechanism reports
##      its result.
##   5. Never simulate physical rollback through ordinary state
##      assignment.
##
## Atomicity:
##   If the mechanism reports success, Observer state must equal
##   destination_state.
##
##   If the mechanism reports failure or is unavailable, Observer
##   state must equal source_state.
##
##   If either invariant is violated, the operation reports an
##   atomicity failure.
##
## TechnologyBoundary:
##   The physical transition mechanism is isolated in
##   physical_transition().
##
##   This function contains the logical transaction semantics and
##   does not invent the physical mechanism.
!#
perform_atomic_transition(observer, target_state):function:internal {

    source_state = observer.state;

    physical_result = physical_transition(
        observer,
        source_state,
        target_state
    );

    if (physical_result.available == false) {

        if (observer.state != source_state) {
            ret TransitionResult(
                success = false,
                source_state = source_state,
                destination_state = target_state,
                failure_reason = "Atomic transition integrity failure: physical mechanism was unavailable but Observer state changed.",
                physical_transition_result = physical_result
            );
        }

        ret TransitionResult(
            success = false,
            source_state = source_state,
            destination_state = target_state,
            failure_reason = physical_result.failure_reason,
            physical_transition_result = physical_result
        );
    }

    if (physical_result.successful == false) {

        if (observer.state != source_state) {
            ret TransitionResult(
                success = false,
                source_state = source_state,
                destination_state = target_state,
                failure_reason = "Atomic transition integrity failure: failed physical transition did not preserve source state.",
                physical_transition_result = physical_result
            );
        }

        ret TransitionResult(
            success = false,
            source_state = source_state,
            destination_state = target_state,
            failure_reason = physical_result.failure_reason,
            physical_transition_result = physical_result
        );
    }

    if (observer.state != target_state) {
        ret TransitionResult(
            success = false,
            source_state = source_state,
            destination_state = target_state,
            failure_reason = "Atomic transition integrity failure: physical mechanism reported success but destination state was not established.",
            physical_transition_result = physical_result
        );
    }

    ret TransitionResult(
        success = true,
        source_state = source_state,
        destination_state = target_state,
        failure_reason = null,
        physical_transition_result = physical_result
    );
}

#! Observer Displacement - Function
##
## ReferenceDefinition:
##   Performs an atomic temporal displacement of an Observer from its
##   current complete state to a specified destination state.
##
## HumanModel:
##   The operation transitions the complete Observer state. It does not
##   modify individual Observer properties independently.
##
## HumanDeclaredReality:
##   A successful displacement places the Observer at target_state.
##   A failed displacement leaves the Observer unchanged at the
##   original source state.
##
## ExpectedBehavior:
##   Inputs:
##     observer:
##       The Observer to be displaced.
##
##     target_state:
##       The complete destination Observer state.
##
##   Validation:
##     Validation occurs internally before execution.
##
##   ReturnBehavior:
##     Returns a DisplacementResult record.
##
## StateIntegrity:
##   No intermediate Observer state is exposed.
!#
displace(observer, target_state):function:external {

    source_state = observer.state;

    validation_result = validate_proposed_displacement(
        observer,
        target_state
    );

    if (validation_result.valid == false) {

        ret DisplacementResult(
            success = false,
            source_state = source_state,
            destination_state = target_state,
            failure_reason = validation_result.failure_reason,
            validation_result = validation_result,
            transition_result = null
        );
    }

    transition_result = perform_atomic_transition(
        observer,
        target_state
    );

    if (transition_result.success == false) {

        ret DisplacementResult(
            success = false,
            source_state = source_state,
            destination_state = target_state,
            failure_reason = transition_result.failure_reason,
            validation_result = validation_result,
            transition_result = transition_result
        );
    }

    ret DisplacementResult(
        success = true,
        source_state = source_state,
        destination_state = target_state,
        failure_reason = null,
        validation_result = validation_result,
        transition_result = transition_result
    );
}


